  "solution": {
    "approach": "The number of digits of a positive integer N is simply the length of its decimal representation. We can obtain it by converting N to a string and taking its length, or by repeatedly dividing N by 10 and counting how many times we can do this before N becomes zero. Both methods run in O(number of digits) time, which is at most 19 for N ≤ 10^18, and use O(1) extra space. The string method is concise and avoids overflow concerns, while the division method works with numeric types directly.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(D) per test case, where D is the number of digits of N (≤ 19). Overall O(T·D).",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) additional space besides the input and output buffers.",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        int T = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());\n        for (int i = 0; i < T; i++) {\n            String line = br.readLine().trim();\n            // N is guaranteed to be a positive integer without leading zeros.\n            int digits = line.length();\n            sb.append(digits);\n            if (i < T - 1) sb.append('\\n');\n        }\n        System.out.print(sb.toString());\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
